The single proposal this cycle targeted k_recovery with a remove edit that deletes trailing prose after a fenced JSON object. The execution shows the check returned the same canonical object before and after the edit, and the expectation was unchanged, so the expectation held. No inputs were thrown away: the cell, input, rewrite, and rewritten text were all echoed in the execution record, and the validator accepted the pair. There are no failures to attribute to a misread rule or to a divergence between the rule as written and the code. The rule says the object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one; the code implements this by collecting fenced candidates and iterating them in reverse, returning the first dict it can parse. The proposal's reasoning matches that behavior. A proposer should build next on a check where the rule and code may part company, such as k_recovered_from_prose, which reports whether recovery was needed even when the strict parse fails for a reason other than prose, or k_span_occurs, where the documented relaxations are never passed by the kernel.